;; mydata.bjodat — the Bjolang data format.
;;
;; The reader reads data and nothing else: no evaluation, no macro expansion,
;; no environment. Every form is a value, so the file is what it says it is.
;;
;; Two differences from source:
;;
;;   - A bare identifier is a Symbol. It is already quoted, because there is
;;     nothing here to evaluate and so nothing for a quote to protect it from.
;;   - `{...}` is a Map. In source a brace opens a comprehension, and a
;;     comprehension is a loop, and a loop is not data.
;;
;; A keyword is written the way source writes it: `:key`, or `#:key`. The two
;; intern to one value, as they do in source.

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; The shapes
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(this is a list of symbols)
["this" "is" "a" "vec" "of" "strings"]
{(:key "value") (:key2 "value2")}

;; A list holds anything, not only symbols, and nests to any depth.
(a "string" 42 :keyword #t (a nested list) [a vec] {(:a map)})

;; There is one sequence type and no `#[...]`. Source splits vec from array
;; because one is immutable and the other is a .NET array, and data is neither:
;; a second spelling would read the same and mean nothing.

;; A comma is not whitespace here the way it is in EDN: source lexes `,` as
;; unquote and `,@` as unquote-splicing, so a stray one is a token, not a
;; decoration. Separate with spaces.

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; The atoms
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

;; A number is an integer or a double: a decimal point or an exponent makes it
;; a double, and `0x` and `0b` are integers written another way. Source's width
;; suffixes — `21uy`, `21L` — are not here. A suffix names a .NET type, and
;; which type a field holds is the record's to say, not the file's.
[42 -100 3.14159 1e10 0x2A 0b101010]

;; `#t` and `#f` are the booleans. `true` and `false` are ordinary identifiers
;; in source, so here they are symbols — a field wanting a boolean wants `#t`.
[#t #f true false]

;; Characters, as in source.
[#\a #\newline #\space]

;; A string escapes as source escapes it. There is no `#"..."` here: a hole
;; holds an expression, an expression needs an environment, and the reader has
;; none.
"a \"quoted\" word, a tab\t, a newline\n, and a backslash \\"

;; The three empties.
[() [] {}]

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Maps
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

;; There are two spellings and no third. Parenthesised, an entry at a time:
{(:paren "a") (:other "b")}

;; Or flat, which is what Clojure writes and what a record wants: the reader
;; sees a key and then its value, and puts the value straight into the field
;; rather than allocating a pair to hold it on the way.
{:name "bjolang/http" :version "1.2.3" :private #f}

;; Which one a map is, is decided by the character after the brace: a `(` means
;; the parenthesised form and anything else means the flat one. They do not mix,
;; and a map that starts one way and continues the other is refused.
;;
;; That rule is why a list may not be a key. If it could, `{(a b) 1}` would be
;; both — a flat map keyed by the list `(a b)`, and a parenthesised map whose
;; first entry is `a -> b`. Refusing list keys leaves one reading, and it costs
;; nothing: a key is a name, and a name is a keyword, a symbol or a string.
;;
;; A flat map with an odd number of forms is the other refusal.

;; A key is any value but a list.
{("a string" 1)
 (a-symbol 2)
 (3 :by-number)
 (#\c :by-char)
 ([a vec] :by-vec)}

;; Entries keep the order they were written in, and writing a map back out
;; writes them in that order. A manifest that round-trips through a record
;; comes back looking like what a person wrote, not sorted by hash.

;; Maps nest, and hold every shape.
{(:deps {(:std "1.0")
         (:text {(:json "2.1")})})
 (:tags [:build :test])
 (:empty {})}

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; A manifest, as a tagged list
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;;
;; Each clause is a list headed by the symbol naming it. It reads like source
;; and diffs a line at a time.
;;
;; It used to say here that this form "says nothing about which clauses may
;; repeat: `requires` holds many, `version` holds one, and only the package
;; manager knows which is which". The first half was right and the conclusion
;; was wrong. The *type* knows, and it is the only thing that could — so it
;; says so, with `#:rest` on the field:
;;
;;   (: depends (List Dep) #:rest)   ;; (depends (package ...) (package ...))
;;   (: version Version)             ;; (version "1.2.3")
;;
;; A field is marked, not a clause repeated: `(depends a) (depends b)` does not
;; accumulate, because the first entry under a key wins for a map and one rule
;; is better than two. See `#:tag` and `#:rest` in `Docs/std/bjodat.org`, and
;; `lib/bjor/manifest.bjo` for the whole of it.

;; A name is a *parenthesised list of symbols* — `(bjolang http)`, `(std run)`
;; — because that is how `import` writes one, and a name written one way is one
;; thing. `bjolang/http` as a single symbol read too, and was a second spelling
;; of the same value with a slash to parse.
;;
;; A version constraint is a form rather than a string. `">=0.4"` is a small
;; language smuggled past the reader: it needs its own parser, its own errors
;; and its own positions, all of which the reader already has.

(package
  (name (bjolang http))
  (version "1.2.3")
  (description "An HTTP client and server.")
  (authors ["Linus" "someone@example.com"])
  (license "MIT")
  (entry "src/main.bjo")
  (targets [net8.0 net9.0])

  (depends
    (package (name (bjolang cml)) (version (version-at-least "2.0.1")))
    (package (name (bjolang tls))
             (version (version-between "0.4" "1.0"))
             (source (git (url "https://github.com/linus/bjo-tls") (branch main))))
    (package (name (bjo core)) (source (path (dir "../bjo-core")))))

  (dev-depends
    (package (name (std simpletest)) (version (version-at-least "1.0")))))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; The same manifest, as a record
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;;
;; A key appears once and the shape says so. Written flat and in the order the
;; record declares its fields, this is what a generated decoder reads fastest:
;; the next key is the one it is expecting, so the check is a pointer compare
;; against an interned keyword and the value goes straight into the field. Out
;; of order still reads — it falls back to a scan over the field names — so a
;; hand-edited file is never refused for its ordering alone.

{:name bjolang/http
 :version "1.2.3"
 :description "An HTTP client and server."
 :authors ["Linus" "someone@example.com"]
 :license "MIT"
 :entry "src/main.bjo"
 :targets [net8.0 net9.0]

 :requires
 {json {:git "repo address here" :version<= "1.3" :commit "abcdeffffff00112233"}
  tls  {:git "https://github.com/linus/bjo-tls" :version>= "0.4" :branch main}
  core {:path "../bjo-core"}
  cml  {:registry "2.0.1"}}

 :dev-requires
 {simpletest {:version>= "1.0"}}

 :scripts
 {:build "bjor build --release"
  :test "bjor test"}}

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Which shape becomes which type
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;;
;; The point of the two spellings above is that they are not rivals: each is the
;; natural reading of a different kind of type, and between them they cover what
;; a manifest declares.
;;
;;   {:field value ...}     a Record, written as a map.
;;   (tag (field value) …)  a Record, written as a tagged form — `#:tag`.
;;   (Ctor arg ...)         a Union case, positionally.
;;   (a b c)                a List — which is what a module name is.
;;   [a b c]                a Vec, homogeneous.
;;   {k v ...}              a Map, when the keys are not field names.
;;
;; This table used to read as though the shape decided. It does not, and that is
;; the load-bearing rule of the whole format: **the expected type picks the
;; shape, and the shape never picks the type.** Four of the six rows above are
;; `(...)`, and they are told apart by what the field asked for, not by anything
;; the reader could see. `(std run)` is a module name where a `(List Symbol)` is
;; wanted and a tagged record where one is; neither reading is privileged and
;; the reader commits to neither.
;;
;; That is the same correspondence `#:literal` uses in source, where a quoted
;; list elaborates into the union case whose payload it fits — driven by the
;; expected type there too. A format that agrees with it means a manifest can be
;; pasted into a `.bjo` and compile.
;;
;; A record decodes without an intermediate map either way: its fields are known
;; when the decoder is generated, so the reader compares each name against them
;; in its own buffer, fills slots as it goes, and calls the constructor once.

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Still open
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;;
;; - Is there a set literal? Source has no set syntax at all — `(std set)` is
;;   reached through functions — so the format would be inventing one.
;; - Does an unknown key refuse, or is it ignored? A manifest format wants to
;;   refuse — a misspelled `:verison` that is silently dropped is a bad day —
;;   but that forbids forward compatibility, so it may want to be a marker.
;;   The reader ignores one today; the codec is where this would be decided.
;;   The same question, and the same answer for now, for an unknown *clause* —
;;   which is skipped at any arity, so that a new clause is not a breaking
;;   change for every older tool.
;; - Writing a nested form back out puts it on one line. `bjodat->string` is the
;;   canonical spelling and a manifest wants an indented one, which is a second
;;   writer and not a second format. Nothing needs it to read.

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Settled
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;;
;; - A file is a *stream* of values. A manifest is one value and a reader asking
;;   for a manifest says so; nothing about the format requires the file to hold
;;   exactly one, which is what makes a log or a lockfile writable by appending.
;; - Flat maps read, beside the paired entries. They do not mix.
;; - A list may not be a key, which is what keeps `{(` unambiguous.
;; - No dotted pairs, no `#[...]`, no width suffixes. Each was a second way to
;;   write something the format already had one way to write.
;; - `'foo` is refused. A symbol is quoted already, so the mark would be a
;;   second spelling of one value, and this file is where that gets decided
;;   rather than discovered.
;; - Map entries keep their written order, in and out.
;; - **The expected type picks the shape.** A `(...)` headed by a symbol is a
;;   tagged record, a union case or a plain list according to the field that
;;   wanted it, and the reader decides none of it.
;; - **Which clauses repeat is a property of the field**, marked `#:rest`. A
;;   clause is not repeated to mean many: `(depends a) (depends b)` takes the
;;   first, because the first entry under a key wins for a map and one rule is
;;   better than two.
;; - A module name is `(bjolang http)` — the parenthesised symbols `import`
;;   writes — and not the string `"bjolang/http"` or the symbol `bjolang/http`.
;; - A version constraint is a form: `(version-at-least "1.2")`,
;;   `(version-at-most "2.0")`, `(version-between "1.2" "2.0")`, all inclusive.
;;   Not `">=1.2"`, which would be a language inside a string with a parser and
;;   an error vocabulary of its own.
